1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,810
Loopback Transformer:

2
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:05,510
Towards a Unified View of
Residual Looped Transformers.

3
00:00:06,910 --> 00:00:10,440
A looped Transformer runs the
same blocks again and again.

4
00:00:10,810 --> 00:00:12,140
That saves parameters,

5
00:00:12,470 --> 00:00:13,620
but with plain addition,

6
00:00:13,830 --> 00:00:16,120
the updates from every pass just pile up.

7
00:00:17,380 --> 00:00:19,010
So we built Loopback.

8
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:23,210
It stores each block's update and
keeps the input on its own path.

9
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:26,230
A decay limits how much old updates count,

10
00:00:26,540 --> 00:00:28,470
and attention picks which ones to read.

11
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:32,520
Here a standard Transformer and Loopback,

12
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:34,020
both at 3B scale,

13
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:35,670
read the same new text.

14
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:37,920
Loopback is 23% smaller,

15
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:39,790
and its loss still ends lower.

16
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:44,310
From 191M to 3B parameters,

17
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:47,310
Loopback gets lower loss than the standard

18
00:00:47,310 --> 00:00:50,050
Transformer with 40–75% of its parameters.

19
00:00:51,810 --> 00:00:53,820
Against seven other looped designs,

20
00:00:54,170 --> 00:00:56,343
it has the lowest loss and the highest

21
00:00:56,343 --> 00:00:59,180
benchmark average at all
three sizes we tested.

22
00:01:01,310 --> 00:01:02,790
And at 3B parameters,

23
00:01:02,790 --> 00:01:05,460
it more than doubles
word-problem accuracy,

24
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:08,480
from 2.6% to 6.4%.

25
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:11,970
That's Loopback.

26
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:12,950
Fewer parameters,

27
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:13,850
lower loss.

28
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:16,250
The paper and code are
on our project page.

